From 2e63fba3adc58902737d85855568c8ecbff42b5d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cristian Deluxe <info@busirocket.com>
Date: Wed, 9 Sep 2026 03:45:35 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 10/17] Input: silead - drive the GSL3673 on the Denver TAQ-102

Route: mainline silead.c, not a port of the vendor driver. This patch adds
the identifiers; the binding is the patch before it and the board node is
in
the board file at the end of the series. It changes no driver logic,
because none is
needed: silead.c and the vendor's gsl3673.c speak the same chip.

Proved by reading both, not by the part number:

  - Register map. The vendor writes 0xE0 = 0x88 to reset, 0xE4 = 0x04 for the
    clock, powers through 0xBC, starts with 0xE0 = 0x00, polls 0xB0 for
    0x5A5A5A5A and reads the id at 0xFC. Every one of those is a SILEAD_REG_*
    in silead.c with the same value.

  - Frame layout. The vendor's data_size is MAX_FINGERS * touch_bytes +
    touch_meta_data = 10 * 4 + 4 = 44, read from 0x80: SILEAD_TS_DATA_LEN. Its
    x_index = 6 / y_index = 4 / touch_bytes = 4 put point i at bytes
    4 + 4i .. 7 + 4i with y at +0, x at +2 and the hardware id in the high
    nibble of x's MSB -- byte for byte silead's SILEAD_POINT_Y_OFF 0,
    SILEAD_POINT_X_OFF 2, SILEAD_POINT_X_MSB_OFF >> 4.

  - Firmware format. The vendor record is

	struct fw_data { u32 offset : 8; u32 : 0; u32 val; };

    where the anonymous zero-width bitfield closes the storage unit, so the
    record is eight bytes with the offset in the low byte of word 0 and the
    value in word 1. Compiled with arm-buildroot-linux-gnueabihf-gcc: sizeof
    == 8, offsetof(val) == 4. That is struct silead_fw_data. So the blob
    silead_ts_load_fw() wants is the vendor's GSL3673_FW array dumped
    little-endian in source order, and kernel/mainline/taq102-gsl3673-firmware.py
    dumps it: 4719 records, 37752 bytes, every offset one of 0x00, 0x04 ...
    0x7c or 0xf0, so no record needs a special case. The only difference in
    the upload is batching -- silead writes one word per transfer where the
    vendor packed up to 32 -- which the chip cannot see.

What is deliberately not carried across: gsl_config_data_id_3673[]. It is
not
firmware and is never written to i2c. Its one non-debug consumer is
startup_chip() -> gsl_DataInit(), inside the closed-source host-side
algorithm
in gsl_point_id.c. silead.c has no such layer and needs none, but it also
does
without what that layer did: gsl_alg_id_main() rewrote coordinates and the
finger count before they were reported, and gsl_mask_tiaoping() wrote a
noise-hopping mask back to the chip at 0xf0/0x08 on every frame. silead
reports the chip's own output and never writes back.

Not proved, because it cannot be proved without the tablet:

  - The chip's unaided output has never been seen on this board. The vendor
    defines GSL_NOID_VERSION in gsl3673.h, so every touch this tablet has ever
    reported went through gsl_alg_id_main() first. The vendor's own
    #ifndef GSL_NOID_VERSION path reads x, y and id straight out of the same
    44-byte frame, which proves the frame carries them; it does not prove they
    are steady enough to use. Watch for jitter, wandering contacts and
    drop-outs with the charger plugged in, which is where a missing frequency
    hop shows first.

  - touchscreen-size-x/y are inferred. The vendor driver's ABS range is not
    evidence: with CONFIG_TOUCHSCREEN_GSL3673 no TP_SIZE_* macro is defined,
    so it falls through to 2048x1536. The 1024x600 here is the resolution word
    of the stock configuration array (0x02580400 = 600 << 16 | 1024) and the
    screen_max_x/y of the reconstructed vendor node. Correcting it is two DTS
    numbers, but a wrong range looks skewed rather than broken.

  - silead masks x and y to 12 bits and reads a non-zero high nibble of the y
    MSB as a soft button: the contact is dropped and KEY_LEFTMETA is emitted.
    The vendor code uses the full 16-bit y and interprets nothing. Harmless
    while y < 0x258, as it must be on a 600-line panel, but if the stock
    firmware puts anything in those bits the touch vanishes silently.

  - The DTS compiles but has never been booted. cpp resolves it and the
    in-tree scripts/dtc turns it into a DTB with no warnings, in which
    touchscreen@40 sits under i2c@2005a000 with interrupts <15 1> and
    touchscreen-size 0x400 x 0x258. Nothing about that says the node is right,
    only that it is well formed.

  - The two regulators are fictions of convenience. Nothing on this board
    switches those rails; they exist because silead_ts_probe() takes "vddio"
    and "avdd" with devm_regulator_bulk_get(), not the _optional form, and
    returns the error. Replace them with the real RK816 outputs when a PMIC
    node lands in this DTS.

One correction to the earlier investigation of this subsystem, which said
i2c0: the touchscreen is on i2c2. In the device tree the tablet actually
runs
(kernel/rk3126-taq102-hybrid.dts, decompiled from the live DTB) ts@40 is a
child of i2c@2005a000, next to the RK816 at 0x1a and the accelerometer at
0x18, while i2c@20072000 is enabled with no children. Both rk3128.dtsi and
the
vendor rk312x.dtsi label 0x2005a000 as i2c2. The live node also carries no
clock-frequency, so the bus runs at the 100 kHz default; the 400 kHz quoted
from rk3126-bnd-d708.dtsi is a different bus on a different board. This
patch
follows the live DTB.

No ACPI id is added: this board has no ACPI, and there is no Silead ACPI
HID
for the GSL3673 that anyone here has observed.

This supersedes 0006-input-silead-add-gsl3673.patch and
0007-arm-dts-rk3126-taq102-gsl3673-touch.patch in the same directory, which
were written against i2c0 at 400 kHz. Apply this one or those, never both.

Kernel side needs CONFIG_TOUCHSCREEN_SILEAD, CONFIG_I2C_RK3X and
CONFIG_REGULATOR_FIXED_VOLTAGE; the rootfs needs the blob at
/lib/firmware/silead/gsl3673.fw.

Signed-off-by: Cristian Deluxe <info@busirocket.com>
---
 drivers/input/touchscreen/silead.c | 2 ++
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/input/touchscreen/silead.c b/drivers/input/touchscreen/silead.c
index 44d714110..c1a6a3a7b 100644
--- a/drivers/input/touchscreen/silead.c
+++ b/drivers/input/touchscreen/silead.c
@@ -779,6 +779,7 @@ static const struct i2c_device_id silead_ts_id[] = {
 	{ .name = "gsl1680" },
 	{ .name = "gsl1688" },
 	{ .name = "gsl3670" },
+	{ .name = "gsl3673" },
 	{ .name = "gsl3675" },
 	{ .name = "gsl3692" },
 	{ .name = "mssl1680" },
@@ -807,6 +808,7 @@ static const struct of_device_id silead_ts_of_match[] = {
 	{ .compatible = "silead,gsl1680" },
 	{ .compatible = "silead,gsl1688" },
 	{ .compatible = "silead,gsl3670" },
+	{ .compatible = "silead,gsl3673" },
 	{ .compatible = "silead,gsl3675" },
 	{ .compatible = "silead,gsl3692" },
 	{ },
